$¬A$ and $¬A∧B:$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,¬A\\,$ | $\\,¬A∧B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n\nNow, we add a column for  $A⇔(¬A∧B):$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,¬A\\,$ | $\\,¬A∧B\\,$ | $\\,A⇔(¬A∧B)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573317 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573317 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182531" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573317 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573317 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A$ is false and $B$ is true, then $A⇔B$ is\n\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573317 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ec004b06-3312-5bf4-be49-682afc34bcbf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573317 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the definition of the biconditional, \n\n- $A⇔B$ is true if $A⇒B$ **and** $B⇒A.$ Otherwise, it is false.\n\n- In other words, $A⇔B$ is true if $A$ and $B$ have the same truth values. Otherwise, it is false.\n\nSo, when $A=\\text{F}$ and $B=\\text{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A⇔B) & =(\\text{F}⇔\\text{T}) \\\\  & =\\text{F}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe complete truth table is shown below:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A⇔B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n|  |  |  |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573323 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573323 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182522" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573323 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573323 #edb/ref 1071 "$¬(B⇔A)≡$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573323 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f686088d-e105-5e19-956f-e84bb86ba487" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573323 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we use the definition of the biconditional statement to write the equivalence as a conjunction of implications: \n\n$$\n¬(B⇔A)≡¬[(B⇒A)∧(A⇒B)]\n$$\n\nNow, we represent all the implications using disjunctions and negations:\n\n$$\n¬[(B⇒A)∧(A⇒B)]≡¬[(¬B∨A)∧(¬A∨B)]\n$$\n\nNext, we use De Morgan's laws:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬[(¬B∨A)∧(¬A∨B)] & ≡¬(¬B∨A)∨¬(¬A∨B) \\\\  & ≡(¬¬B∧¬A)∨(¬¬A∧¬B)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the double negations and write down symbols in alphabetic order inside the parentheses using commutative laws:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(¬¬B∧¬A)∨(¬¬A∧¬B) & ≡(B∧¬A)∨(A∧¬B) \\\\  & ≡(¬A∧B)∨(A∧¬B)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
